      "content": "The argument that social safety net programs can create disincentives is a well-documented part of the political and economic debate, often related to \"poverty traps.\" Data supporting this claim often focuses on how benefit phase-outs can lead to a sharp loss of support as a very small increase in earned income is made, effectively disincentivizing the first small steps into the formal labor market.\n\nHowever, this is not the only side of the argument, and the impact heavily depends on the *design* of the specific program, such as the benefit cliff and the level of support provided for education or early care.",
